1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,317
On a calendar, every
Sunday sits in one column.

2
00:00:03,317 --> 00:00:06,424
In a cinema, you find your
seat by row and number.

3
00:00:06,424 --> 00:00:09,539
In a tall building, a flat
is a floor and a door.

4
00:00:09,939 --> 00:00:13,035
Rows and columns turn a
crowd into an address.

5
00:00:13,035 --> 00:00:15,609
The periodic table works the same way.

6
00:00:16,059 --> 00:00:18,894
Diya pins a calendar
above her study table.

7
00:00:18,894 --> 00:00:21,620
Seven days across, the weeks going down.

8
00:00:21,970 --> 00:00:25,067
The periodic table also
has rows and columns.

9
00:00:25,067 --> 00:00:28,113
But why these rows, and why these columns?

10
00:00:28,613 --> 00:00:32,753
By the end of this video, you will
know why one row has two elements, and

11
00:00:32,753 --> 00:00:34,150
another has thirty two.

12
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:37,372
So what does the modern table look like?

13
00:00:37,372 --> 00:00:39,219
It is called the long form.

14
00:00:39,569 --> 00:00:41,305
The rows are called periods.

15
00:00:41,305 --> 00:00:42,862
There are seven of them.

16
00:00:43,212 --> 00:00:44,945
The columns are called groups.

17
00:00:44,945 --> 00:00:47,498
There are eighteen,
numbered one to eighteen.

18
00:00:47,898 --> 00:00:50,269
This numbering is the IUPAC system.

19
00:00:50,269 --> 00:00:53,684
It replaced older labels
like one A, and seven B.

20
00:00:54,084 --> 00:00:56,271
Two short rows sit at the bottom.

21
00:00:56,271 --> 00:01:00,541
They are the lanthanoids and the
actinoids, kept apart so the table does

22
00:01:00,541 --> 00:01:01,805
not get too wide.

23
00:01:02,455 --> 00:01:06,676
Elements in one group have the same
outer electron arrangement, so they

24
00:01:06,676 --> 00:01:07,650
behave alike.

25
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:10,687
Along a period, properties
change step by step.

26
00:01:11,337 --> 00:01:12,830
Now the big question.

27
00:01:12,830 --> 00:01:15,263
Why do periods have different lengths?

28
00:01:15,263 --> 00:01:17,947
Let's fill electrons
on our molecule bench.

29
00:01:18,297 --> 00:01:21,910
The period number is the
shell being filled, called n.

30
00:01:21,910 --> 00:01:24,147
Period one fills the first shell.

31
00:01:24,147 --> 00:01:27,048
Only one s orbital, so only two electrons.

32
00:01:27,448 --> 00:01:29,146
Hydrogen, then helium.

33
00:01:29,146 --> 00:01:30,595
The shell is full.

34
00:01:30,595 --> 00:01:33,325
Period one ends with just two elements.

35
00:01:33,725 --> 00:01:35,692
Period two fills shell two.

36
00:01:35,692 --> 00:01:38,891
One two s orbital, and
three two p orbitals.

37
00:01:38,891 --> 00:01:40,537
That is four orbitals.

38
00:01:40,937 --> 00:01:43,157
Each orbital holds two electrons.

39
00:01:43,157 --> 00:01:44,922
Four times two is eight.

40
00:01:44,922 --> 00:01:48,343
So period two has eight
elements, lithium to neon.

41
00:01:48,743 --> 00:01:51,522
Period three fills three s and three p.

42
00:01:51,522 --> 00:01:55,609
Again four orbitals, again
eight elements, sodium to argon.

43
00:01:56,259 --> 00:01:58,325
Period four adds a new set.

44
00:01:58,325 --> 00:02:01,099
Four s, then three d, then four p.

45
00:02:01,099 --> 00:02:04,083
One plus five plus three is nine orbitals.

46
00:02:04,483 --> 00:02:06,348
Nine times two is eighteen.

47
00:02:06,348 --> 00:02:10,678
That is why period four has eighteen
elements, potassium to krypton.

48
00:02:10,678 --> 00:02:13,152
The d orbitals fill the middle block.

49
00:02:13,802 --> 00:02:14,826
Now compare.

50
00:02:14,826 --> 00:02:17,475
Same group means same outer electrons.

51
00:02:17,475 --> 00:02:19,965
Same period means same outer shell.

52
00:02:20,365 --> 00:02:21,624
Pause and think.

53
00:02:21,624 --> 00:02:23,722
Which pair behaves more alike?

54
00:02:23,722 --> 00:02:26,864
Sodium and potassium,
or sodium and magnesium?

55
00:02:30,064 --> 00:02:31,616
Sodium and potassium!

56
00:02:31,616 --> 00:02:35,223
They share group one, with
one outer electron each.

57
00:02:35,223 --> 00:02:36,617
Magnesium has two.

58
00:02:37,267 --> 00:02:38,332
Remember this.

59
00:02:38,332 --> 00:02:40,123
A period fills one shell.

60
00:02:40,123 --> 00:02:42,570
A group shares one outer arrangement.

61
00:02:43,270 --> 00:02:45,006
Now, one worked example.

62
00:02:45,306 --> 00:02:47,545
How many elements are in period six?

63
00:02:48,045 --> 00:02:48,945
Step one.

64
00:02:48,931 --> 00:02:51,991
List the orbitals that fill in period six.

65
00:02:51,991 --> 00:02:54,673
Six s, four f, five d, six p.

66
00:02:55,423 --> 00:02:56,328
Step two.

67
00:02:56,328 --> 00:02:57,344
Count them.

68
00:02:57,344 --> 00:03:00,247
One s, seven f, five d, three p.

69
00:03:00,247 --> 00:03:02,168
That is sixteen orbitals.

70
00:03:02,918 --> 00:03:03,907
Step three.

71
00:03:03,907 --> 00:03:05,830
Two electrons per orbital.

72
00:03:05,830 --> 00:03:08,205
Sixteen times two is thirty two.

73
00:03:08,955 --> 00:03:11,330
So period six has thirty two elements.

74
00:03:11,330 --> 00:03:14,935
Fourteen of them, the lanthanoids,
sit in the bottom row.

75
00:03:15,585 --> 00:03:17,051
Now, the exam trap.

76
00:03:17,401 --> 00:03:20,998
Many students say period three
has two n squared elements.

77
00:03:20,998 --> 00:03:23,309
Two times three squared is eighteen.

78
00:03:23,709 --> 00:03:28,083
But period three fills only three
s and three p, so it holds eight.

79
00:03:28,083 --> 00:03:31,252
The three d orbitals fill
later, in period four.

80
00:03:31,902 --> 00:03:33,204
Where is this asked?

81
00:03:33,204 --> 00:03:37,388
As short questions on the number of
elements in a period, and which orbitals

82
00:03:37,388 --> 00:03:38,288
fill in it.

83
00:03:38,844 --> 00:03:40,004
Exam level now.

84
00:03:40,004 --> 00:03:44,348
How many elements are in the fifth
period, and which orbitals fill?

85
00:03:44,348 --> 00:03:45,557
Pause and solve.

86
00:03:50,957 --> 00:03:52,319
The answer is B.

87
00:03:52,319 --> 00:03:56,227
Eighteen elements, filling
five s, four d and five p.

88
00:03:56,727 --> 00:03:58,750
Period five fills shell five.

89
00:03:58,750 --> 00:04:01,298
It starts with five s, one orbital.

90
00:04:01,798 --> 00:04:05,759
Then four d, five orbitals,
and five p, three orbitals.

91
00:04:05,759 --> 00:04:07,324
Nine orbitals in all.

92
00:04:07,824 --> 00:04:09,859
Nine times two is eighteen.

93
00:04:09,859 --> 00:04:12,729
D uses two n squared, which is fifty.

94
00:04:12,729 --> 00:04:15,761
The four f orbitals wait until period six.

95
00:04:16,361 --> 00:04:17,820
Here is your recap.

96
00:04:17,820 --> 00:04:19,319
Screenshot this card.

97
00:04:22,519 --> 00:04:25,356
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

98
00:04:25,756 --> 00:04:26,656
One.

99
00:04:26,237 --> 00:04:29,495
How many periods and groups
does the long form have?

100
00:04:30,296 --> 00:04:31,196
Two.

101
00:04:30,843 --> 00:04:34,206
Why does period two have
exactly eight elements?

102
00:04:35,005 --> 00:04:35,905
Three.

103
00:04:35,635 --> 00:04:39,558
Why are the lanthanoids and
actinoids placed at the bottom?

104
00:04:40,457 --> 00:04:44,016
Next time: what makes an
element s, p, d, or f?

105
00:04:44,016 --> 00:04:46,865
We find out in periods, groups and blocks.
